WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.153
Scattered fetch calls make destination
policy inconsistent and difficult to audit.

2
00:00:05.233 --> 00:00:08.723
A narrow outbound-request service can
centralize URL parsing,

3
00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:12.809
named destination policies, DNS and
address checks, redirect handling,

4
00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:16.250
resource budgets, credential rules, and
sanitized logging.

5
00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:19.238
Avoid a magical helper that silently
guesses intent.

6
00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:22.549
Each caller should select a specific
policy and operation,

7
00:00:22.629 --> 00:00:27.346
and the service should return constrained
results rather than exposing unrestricted

8
00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:28.331
client options.

9
00:00:28.411 --> 00:00:31.836
Code review should trace the destination
from input to socket.

10
00:00:31.916 --> 00:00:35.276
Inventory Fetch, Axios-style clients,
Undici dispatchers,

11
00:00:35.356 --> 00:00:38.523
webhook senders, importers, preview
services, proxies,

12
00:00:38.603 --> 00:00:40.800
and raw or alternate-protocol clients.

13
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.948
Confirm where the target originates,

14
00:00:43.028 --> 00:00:45.419
whether an arbitrary URL is truly
required,

15
00:00:45.499 --> 00:00:49.183
which protocols and ports are allowed, how
hostnames are compared,

16
00:00:49.263 --> 00:00:51.201
which resolver the transport uses,

17
00:00:51.281 --> 00:00:55.805
whether every candidate address is
classified, and what happens after redirects.

18
00:00:55.885 --> 00:00:59.504
Review credentials, proxy behavior,
timeouts, response limits,

19
00:00:59.584 --> 00:01:01.975
and the networks reachable by the
workload.

20
00:01:02.055 --> 00:01:06.708
Tests should exercise policy boundaries
without becoming an exploitation catalog.

21
00:01:06.788 --> 00:01:10.665
Cover malformed URLs, unsupported
protocols, embedded credentials,

22
00:01:10.745 --> 00:01:15.010
forbidden ports, direct IP literals,
loopback and private classifications,

23
00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.515
IPv6 and mapped forms, mixed DNS results,
resolution failure,

24
00:01:18.595 --> 00:01:22.666
redirects to denied destinations, redirect
loops, and excessive chains.

25
00:01:22.746 --> 00:01:26.947
Use controlled test resolvers and
transports so results are deterministic.

26
00:01:27.027 --> 00:01:31.292
Verify that the connection layer receives
only an approved address and that an

27
00:01:31.372 --> 00:01:34.409
independently changing answer cannot
bypass the test.

28
00:01:34.489 --> 00:01:38.431
Also test connection timeouts, oversized
or over-expanded responses,

29
00:01:38.511 --> 00:01:41.549
and cross-tenant attempts to use stored
integrations.

30
00:01:41.629 --> 00:01:46.152
Observability should distinguish parse
rejection, destination-policy denial,

31
00:01:46.232 --> 00:01:50.756
DNS failure, connection failure, redirect
denial, resource-limit termination,

32
00:01:50.836 --> 00:01:54.455
and upstream error without revealing
secrets or response bodies.

33
00:01:54.535 --> 00:01:58.541
Metrics can identify repeated denials or
unexpected destination drift,

34
00:01:58.621 --> 00:02:01.982
while alerts should be tuned to the
service's risk and volume.

35
00:02:02.062 --> 00:02:06.521
Centralization, tests, review, and
telemetry do not replace a sound destination

36
00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:06.989
model.

37
00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:09.460
They make that model visible, enforceable,

38
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.900
and maintainable as libraries and
deployment routes evolve.
